<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-barry-harris" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Barry Harris and Jazz Piano Theory</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This chapter presents the harmonic and theoretical system developed by the Detroit 
      jazz pianist and educator Barry Harris (1929–2021). Harris spent more than six 
      decades teaching jazz in workshops around the world, and his approach offers one 
      of the most coherent and practical frameworks for understanding bebop harmony ever 
      assembled. His system is centred on two complementary ideas: the <term>bebop scale</term> 
      and the <term>6th chord / diminished 7th system</term>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Although Barry Harris was primarily a pianist, his theories describe universal 
      properties of tonal harmony and are equally applicable to any chromatic instrument. 
      For the accordion player, the isomorphic layout of the chromatic button keyboard is 
      a particular advantage: every pattern, every scale, every block-chord exercise has 
      exactly the same fingering in all twelve keys. The daily exercises Harris recommended 
      in <em>every</em> key can be mastered more systematically on the accordion than on 
      most other instruments.
    </p>
    <p>
      The chapter is organised as follows: the introduction presents Harris's background 
      and philosophy; this is followed by a detailed treatment of the three bebop scales 
      with notation; the 6th chord / diminished 7th system is then explained both 
      theoretically and in terms of four-voice keyboard voicings; and finally, a set of 
      daily scale and chord exercises — drawn directly from Harris's workshop teaching — 
      is presented with practice guidance.
    </p>
  </introduction>

